[s0l0!-header][h1]var & let[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

In ES6 we have three ways of declaring variables: [code format="js" ][b]var[/b] a = 10;
[b]const[/b] b = 'hello';
[b]let[/b] c = true;[/code]
The type of declaration used depends on the necessary [b]scope[/b]. [b]Scope [/b]is the fundamental concept in all programming languages that defines the visibility of a variable.

[b]var[/b] & [b]let[/b]
Unlike the [b]var [/b]keyword, which defines a variable globally, or locally to an entire function regardless of block scope, [b]let [/b]allows you to declare variables that are limited in scope to the block, statement, or expression in which they are used. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header]  [code format="js" codeId="1416"]if (true) {
  [b]let[/b] name = 'Jack';
}
alert(name); //generates an error [/code]
In this case, the [b]name [/b]variable is accessible only in the scope of the [b]if [/b]statement because it was declared as [b]let[/b].

To demonstrate the difference in scope between [b]var [/b]and [b]let[/b], consider this example: [code format="js" codeId="1428"]function varTest() {
  [b]var [/b]x = 1;
  if (true) {
    [b]var [/b]x = 2;  // same variable
    console.log(x);  // 2
  }
  console.log(x);  // 2
}

function letTest() {
  [b]let [/b]x = 1;
  if (true) {
    [b]let [/b]x = 2;  // different variable
    console.log(x);  // 2
  }
  console.log(x);  // 1
}[/code]
One of the best uses for [b]let [/b]is in loops: [code format="js" codeId="1417"]for ([b]let [/b]i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  document.write(i);
}[/code]
Here, the [b]i[/b] variable is accessible only within the scope of the [b]for [/b]loop, where it is needed. 
[note][b]let[/b] is not subject to [b]Variable Hoisting[/b], which means that [b]let [/b]declarations do not move to the top of the current execution context.[/note]

