[s0l0!-header][h1]Template Literals in ES6[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

[b]Template literals[/b] are a way to output variables in the string. 
Prior to ES6 we had to break the string, for example: [code format="js" codeId="1439"]let name = 'David';
let msg = 'Welcome ' + name + '!';
console.log(msg);[/code]
ES6 introduces a new way of outputting variable values in strings. The same code above can be rewritten as: [code format="js" codeId="1440"]let name = 'David';
let msg = `Welcome [b]${name}[/b]!`;
console.log(msg);[/code]
Notice, that template literals are enclosed by the [b]backtick [/b](` `) character instead of double or single quotes.
The [b]${expression}[/b] is a placeholder, and can include any expression, which will get evaluated and inserted into the template literal.

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1441"]let a = 8;
let b = 34;
let msg = `The sum is [b]${a+b}[/b]`;
console.log(msg);[/code]
[note]To escape a backtick in a template literal, put a backslash \ before the backtick.[/note]
